Let $y$ be the roots of the second equation. Then the roots of $x^2 + (m-14)x + (49 - 7m + n) = 0$ are twice the roots of this. So we have \begin{align*}(2y_1)^2 + (m-14)(2y_1) + (49 - 7m + n) &= 0 \\ 4y_1^2 + (2m-28)y_1 + (49 - 7m + n) &= 0.\end{align*}But this is simply twice  $$y_1^2 + (m-14)y_1 + (49 - 7m + n),$$which must therefore be a constant multiple of $$y_1^2 + (m-14)y_1 + (49 - 7m + n),$$for all $y_1$. It follows that $$y_1^2 + (m-14)y_1 + (49 - 7m + n)$$and $$y_1^2 + (p-14)y_1 + (49 - 7p + m)$$must represent the same quadratic terms. Using Vieta, since their constants are not equal, $$\frac{49 - 7m + n}{49 - 7p + m} = 1.$$Now $\frac{49 - 7m + n}{49 - 7p + m}= \frac{49 - 7m + n}{7(7 - p) - m},$ so $$\frac{49 - 7m + n}{49 - 7p + m} - 1 = -\frac{14m - 7n}{49 - 7p + m} = 0.$$Thus, we must have $14m = 7n,$ so $n/p = \boxed{\frac{2}{1}}$. $\square$ </think> <answer> \frac{2}{1} </answer>

